================================================================================================
THE CODE — the same write-back, with and without one check
================================================================================================

  VULNERABLE                              HARDENED
  ----------------------------            ----------------------------
  new = u256(balance - amount)            if amount > balance:
  store(addr, new)                            revert("insufficient")
                                          store(addr, balance - amount)

  In Solidity 0.8 the compiler inserts that check for you.
  In a Go precompile below the EVM, nobody does. You write it, or it isn't there.


  1. STATE BEFORE  (attacker holds exactly 2 KII)
    attacker                                                       2,000,000,000,000,000,000
    victim                                                   900,000,000,000,000,000,000,000

================================================================================================
2. THE SUBTRACTION — delegate 2 KII + ONE WEI
================================================================================================
    balance                                                       2,000,000,000,000,000,000
  - amount                                                        2,000,000,000,000,000,001
  = true                                                                                 -1   <- negative. There is no negative.
  = STORED   115,792,089,237,316,195,423,570,985,008,687,907,853,269,984,665,640,564,039,457,584,007,913,129,639,935

     STATE AFTER
    attacker  115,792,089,237,316,195,423,570,985,008,687,907,853,269,984,665,640,564,039,457,584,007,913,129,639,935
    victim                                                   900,000,000,000,000,000,000,000

================================================================================================
3. "SO WHAT?" — because that number IS the ledger
================================================================================================
  Every downstream check reads it and answers honestly:

    require(balance >= 1,000,000,000,000,000,000               )   ->   PASS   can he send 1 KII?
    require(balance >= 1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000       )   ->   PASS   can he send 1,000,000 KII?
    require(balance >= 1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000)   ->   PASS   can he send 10^30 KII?

  Nothing is broken. Nothing is bypassed. The check works perfectly
  and returns the right answer about a number that is a lie.

================================================================================================
4. "OK, THE VICTIM IS AT ZERO. WHAT DO I GET?"
================================================================================================
  Nothing — if it stopped there. A wrapped balance in your own row is not money,
  and destroying someone else's row does not pay you either.

  What KiiChain's post-mortem actually describes is a DEBIT/CREDIT PAIR:
  the same underflowed delegation debits a victim address AND credits the
  attacker's helper address with the same amount. Value moves, it is not burned.


     LEDGER AFTER ONE ROUND (attacker row = real tokens, not a wrapped number)
    attacker                                                 900,000,000,000,000,000,000,000
    victim                                                                                 0

  KiiChain: this exact workflow was repeated 18 times against different targets.
  Total moved: 148,326,583.15 KII

================================================================================================
5. AND *THIS* IS THE PAYDAY — the part that happens off this chain
================================================================================================
    helper address -> attacker's primary KiiChain address a second EVM tx to the same helper contract
    bridge OUT via Hyperlane to BNB Smart Chain          67,597,997.87 KII  (45.6%)
    sell on PancakeSwap                                  64,597,997.87 KII into the pool
    deposit to a CEX                                     3,000,000 KII to a KuCoin address
    REALISED                                             about $1,600,000

  Inflated KII on KiiChain is a number in a database the victims' own chain controls.
  It only becomes money once it is somewhere that chain cannot reach, sold to
  somebody who pays in an asset nobody can freeze.

  Proof that this is the real bottleneck: KiiChain halted at block 9355723 and
  80,728,575.06 KII - 54.4% of the theft - never left the chain and is still frozen.
  Same exploit, same balances. The half that had not been SOLD yet was worth nothing.

